<script>
  import Aside from '$lib/components/Aside.svelte';
  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

In the summer of 2024, reporters at Gothamist published ["NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year"](https://gothamist.com/news/hot-spots-nypd-data-shows-most-shootings-occur-on-the-same-blocks-year-after-year), a data-driven investigation into gun violence in New York City.

The analysis, by reporters Brittany Kriegstein and Jaclyn Jeffrey-Wilensky, showed that shooting incidents were not, as some might assume, a widespread problem that reached every corner of the city.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/gothamist-story.png"
  alt="Gothamist's 'Hot spots: NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year' story page"
  chromeTitle="Hot spots: NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year"
  chromeUrl="https://gothamist.com/news/hot-spots-nypd-data-shows-most-shootings-occur-on-the-same-blocks-year-after-year"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Instead, by downloading and mapping the data published to [the city's open data portal](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Public-Safety/NYPD-Shooting-Incident-Data-Historic-/833y-fsy8/data_preview), they found that gunfire was concentrated in a small number of areas, with nine areas accounting for a massive share of shootings.

"Just 4% of New York City’s 120,000 blocks account for nearly all of the city's shootings," the story reported. "Life even two or three blocks away from these nine hot spots can be dramatically different, with less violence, fewer crimes and residents who say they feel relatively safe."

Alongside the story, Gothamist published [an interactive map](https://gothamist.com/news/new-york-city-gun-violence-map) that allowed readers to see their findings and explore the data for themselves. It shaded every Census block in the five boroughs by how many shooting incidents the NYPD recorded there over the previous four years.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/gothamist-gun-violence.png"
  alt="Gothamist's 'Map: Where New York City's shootings happen' showing a map of the five boroughs shaded by census block, with the darkest blocks concentrated in parts of the Bronx, Brooklyn, and Harlem"
  chromeTitle="Map: Where New York City's shootings happen"
  chromeUrl="https://gothamist.com/news/new-york-city-gun-violence-map"
/>

Their work follows a common model for data journalism: Mapping a dataset to reveal patterns and steer reporting, then publishing an interactive version to let readers place themselves in the story, regardless of where they live.

Today you are going to learn how to do just that. We'll cover how to plot points, shade areas and invite users to interact with the data.

Like Gothamist, our source will be the New York City data portal. But instead of crime, we'll be mapping the city's massive population of trees.

## Part 1: Making your first map

The first step is getting a bare map on the page. Let's begin.

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/template-repo-homepage.png"
  alt="GitHub template repository page showing the Use this template button"
  chromeTitle="cuny-jour-static-site-template"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template"
/>

Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `nyc-tree-map`. Clone it, open it in Visual Studio Code, and install the dependencies.

```bash
npm install
```

Start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

And open [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) in your browser.

### Meet MapLibre

[MapLibre GL JS](https://maplibre.org/) is an open-source library for rendering vector maps in the browser. Give it some coordinates, a zoom level and a style for its base map, and it draws a fully interactive map. It's free to use, it integrates nicely with SvelteKit and, thus, it powers many of the maps you see on news sites.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/maplibre-homepage.png"
  alt="The MapLibre homepage describing the open-source mapping library"
  chromeTitle="MapLibre"
  chromeUrl="https://maplibre.org/"
/>

MapLibre has an extensive set of Node.JS tools governing every aspect of map rendering and interaction. Putting together a map like the one Gothamist publishes can require writing hundreds of lines of carefully constructed code. It can be a little daunting.

Rather than force everyone to climb the learning curve and rewrite similar code snippets for every project, well organized newsrooms build reusable components that encapsulate the most common pattern. That way, a newsroom developer can drop a map on the page with a few lines of code, and then customize it as needed.

As in past weeks, your class template already includes the components we'll use this week. Like the rest of the library, they're documented on the [Storybook](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/) site.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/storybook-map-components.png"
  alt="The class template's Storybook showing the Map, MapLayer, and Geocoder components in the sidebar"
  chromeTitle="Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/compositions-interactive-map--docs"
/>

The key ones this week are:

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| [`Map`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/maps-map--docs) | The container for your map, as well as its base configuration. |
| [`MapLayer`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/maps-maplayer--docs) | A single data layer. Drop it inside `<Map>` to plot points, lines and polygons. |
| [`Geocoder`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/maps-geocoder--docs) | A search box that turns an address into coordinates.  |

Open Storybook and poke at each one before moving on.

### Creating a blank map

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace the boilerplate with this:

```svelte
<script>
  import ArticleHeader from '$lib/components/Article/ArticleHeader.svelte';
  import Map from '$lib/components/maps/Map.svelte';
</script>

<div class="container">
  <ArticleHeader
    headline="The Trees of New York"
    byline="NYCity News Service"
    pubDate="2026-04-20"
  />

  <Map
    longitude={-74.0}
    latitude={40.7}
    zoom={9.5}
    height={600}
    theme="positron"
    credit="OpenFreeMap / OpenStreetMap contributors"
  />
</div>
```

Save the file and check your browser. You should see a clean pale-gray map of New York City in our standard template.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/bare-map.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of New York City with no data overlaid, sitting below an article header reading 'The Trees of New York' with a byline"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
/>

Play with it a little. Drag the map around. Zoom in and out with the scroll wheel. Then try changing the `theme` prop to `"liberty"` or `"bright"` and save — the basemap should swap without you reloading the page.

## Part 2: Putting points on the map

In 1985, the New York City Parks Department began an ambitious new venture. They called it the Great Tree Search, where the agency called on the public to nominate "trees of unusual size, species, form or historical association." Volunteers from all five boroughs wrote in with trees of unusual size, rare species, strange form, or deep historical association.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/nyc-parks-great-trees.png"
  alt="The NYC Parks Great Trees page listing the full roster with photos, species, and neighborhoods"
  chromeTitle="Great Trees of New York City"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nycgovparks.org/facilities/great-trees"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Forty years later, an updated roster listed 119 noteworthy trees, each a small piece of New York history.

I scraped those entries from the city website and converted them to the format preferred by MapLibre: GeoJSON.

[GeoJSON](https://geojson.org/) is a standard format for encoding geographic data. It looks like regular JSON, but with a specific structure: a `FeatureCollection` containing `Feature` objects, each with `properties` and `geometry` that describe what the thing is and where it sits on the map.

Here's one example from the file:

```json
{
  "type": "Feature",
  "geometry": {
    "type": "Point",
    "coordinates": [-73.9843, 40.7342]
  },
  "properties": {
    "id": 34,
    "species": "English elm",
    "description": "Western gate of Stuyvesant Square Park",
    "image": "https://www.nycgovparks.org/images/facilities/uploads/small/54dd2a39155b4.jpg"
  }
}
```

Notice the coordinates are `[longitude, latitude]` with the x-axis position first. This is the standard for GeoJSON, but it's the opposite of how some other mapping programs store geographic data. Be sure you get that order right when you build your own GeoJSON files.

We'll fetch the file using the same [`+page.js`](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/routing#page-page-js) load function pattern you've used in previous weeks.

First download the file I've prepared from [this link](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/palewire/ecb68c32c9172ba76d9a07e701de0cbd/raw/ccf6c6fdd801b4dd04119a3d33d87e9145d21f24/nyc-great-trees.geojson) and place it in `lib/data/great-trees.json` in your project.

Create `src/routes/+page.js`:

```js
import greatTrees from '$lib/data/great-trees.json';

export function load() {
  return {
    showHeader: true,
    showFooter: true,
    greatTrees,
  };
}
```

Now, as in past weeks, we can add a `$props` line to our script in `+page.svelte` to receive the data.

Update the `<script>` block in `+page.svelte` to receive it:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5-7"}
<script>
  import ArticleHeader from '$lib/components/Article/ArticleHeader.svelte';
  import Map from '$lib/components/maps/Map.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
  const greatTrees = data.greatTrees;
  console.log(greatTrees);
</script>
```

Refresh the page and open the browser's developer console. You should see the full GeoJSON object logged there, with a `features` array containing 119 entries.

### Adding a layer

The template's `MapLayer` component takes a GeoJSON collection and a MapLibre paint object and renders the data on the parent map. You want to import it and drop it as a child of `<Map>`, which tells MapLibre to render it as a layer on top of the basemap.

Here's a fully functioning example with some simple styles and a new top to the page.

```svelte
<script>
  import ArticleHeader from '$lib/components/Article/ArticleHeader.svelte';
  import Map from '$lib/components/maps/Map.svelte';
  import MapLayer from '$lib/components/maps/MapLayer.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
  const greatTrees = data.greatTrees;
  console.log(greatTrees);
</script>

<div class="container">
  <ArticleHeader
    headline="The Great Trees of New York"
    byline="NYCity News Service"
    pubDate="2026-04-20"
  />

  <p>
    In 1985, the New York City Parks Department began an ambitious new venture.
    They called it the Great Tree Search.
  </p>

  <p>
    The agency called on the public to nominate "trees of unusual size, species,
    form or historical association." Volunteers from all five boroughs wrote in
    with trees of unusual size, rare species, strange form, or deep historical
    association. Explore them here and see if you can find any near you.
  </p>

  <Map
    longitude={-74.0}
    latitude={40.7}
    zoom={9.5}
    height={600}
    theme="positron"
    credit="OpenFreeMap / OpenStreetMap contributors"
  >
    <MapLayer
      id="great-trees"
      type="circle"
      data={greatTrees}
      paint={{
        'circle-color': '#0033a1',
        'circle-radius': 5,
        'circle-stroke-color': '#1a1a1a',
        'circle-stroke-width': 1,
        'circle-opacity': 0.9,
      }}
    />
  </Map>
</div>
```

Save. Your map should now be studded with blue dots.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/great-tree-dots.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of the five boroughs studded with blue dots"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The `type` property in `MapLayer` tells MapLibre how to draw the data. Since our GeoJSON features are points, we use the `circle` type, which draws a simple circle at each point's coordinates. If our data had lines or polygons, we would use the `line` or `fill` types instead.

The `data` property is where you pass the GeoJSON collection. MapLibre reads the coordinates and properties of each feature and draws them on the map.

They're styled by the `paint` object you passed to `MapLayer`. MapLibre has a huge catalog of styling options for every type of layer. The ones we used here are:

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `circle-color` | The fill color of each point. |
| `circle-radius` | The size of each point, in pixels. |
| `circle-stroke-color` | The color of the outline around each point. |
| `circle-stroke-width` | The width of the outline, in pixels. |
| `circle-opacity` | The opacity of the whole point, from 0 to 1. |

You can experiment with other paint properties in the [MapLibre Style Specification](https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/layers/#layer-properties) to customize the look of your points. Try tweaking the color, size and opacity to see how it changes the map.

### Adding a popup

The dots are nice, but they don't tell you anything about the trees. In practice, most maps offer a way to click on a feature and get more information about it.

Our `MapLayer` component accepts a `popup` property that will handle all the code for you. All you need to do is provide a function that takes a feature and returns the HTML you want to show in the popup.

Add a simple `popup` to your `MapLayer`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="12-18"}
  <MapLayer
    id="great-trees"
    type="circle"
    data={greatTrees}
    paint={{
      'circle-color': '#0033a1',
      'circle-radius': 5,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#1a1a1a',
      'circle-stroke-width': 1,
      'circle-opacity': 0.9,
    }}
    popup={(feature) => {
      const p = feature.properties;
      return `<strong>${p.species}</strong><br>${p.description}`;
    }}
  />
```

MapLayer calls the function every time a dot is clicked and passes it the full feature, so you can build whatever HTML you'd like around the data. Keep it simple for now: the species on top, the location underneath.

Save. Click one of the blue dots. A popup should appear.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/great-tree-popup.png"
  alt="A zoomed-in map showing a blue dot over Prospect Park with a popup reading 'Camperdown elm' above a line about its location south of the Boathouse"
  chromeTitle="The Great Trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

## Part 3: Searching by location

A zoomed map centered on the whole city is fine for seeing the overall distribution, but it doesn't make it easy for readers to find the trees near them. We can solve that problem by adding a search box that lets readers type in an address and fly the map to that location.

The process of turning a human-readable place, like Kips Bay or Flushing, into coordinates pinned to a map is a common problem in cartography. The technical term for it is geocoding. Every interactive map with a search box you have ever used is leaning on a geocoder under the hood to turn your query into a point on the map.

Most newsrooms pay a commercial geocoding provider like Google Maps or Mapbox for this service, but there are also free and open-source geocoders available that do the same thing without charging you for every search. They just aren't as good.

The class template uses [Nominatim](https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/), the open-source geocoder run by the OpenStreetMap project. It's free to use at modest volumes with no API key required.

The template's [`Geocoder`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/maps-geocoder--docs) component wraps it in a styled search box.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/geocoder-storybook.png"
  alt="The Storybook documentation page for the Geocoder component"
  chromeTitle="Geocoder – Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/maps-geocoder--docs"
/>

When a user picks a result, `Geocoder` fires an `onresult` callback with its coordinates. All you have to do is hand those coordinates to our `Map`.

You can do that in `+page.svelte`. Import the component and add reactive state variables for the coordinates and zoom:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5,9-11"}
<script>
  import ArticleHeader from '$lib/components/Article/ArticleHeader.svelte';
  import Map from '$lib/components/maps/Map.svelte';
  import MapLayer from '$lib/components/maps/MapLayer.svelte';
  import Geocoder from '$lib/components/maps/Geocoder.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
  const greatTrees = data.greatTrees;
  let longitude = $state(-74.0);
  let latitude = $state(40.7);
  let zoom = $state(9.5);
</script>
```

Now replace the hard-coded numbers in the `<Map>` tag with those state variables, and add a `<Geocoder>` above the map with a callback that updates them.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3-10,14-16"}
  <p>
    The agency called on the public to nominate "trees of unusual size, species,
    form or historical association." Volunteers from all five boroughs wrote in
    with trees of unusual size, rare species, strange form, or deep historical
    association. Explore them here and see if you can find any near you.
  </p>

  <Geocoder
    label="Find your neighborhood"
    placeholder="Enter an address in New York…"
    onresult={(result) => {
      longitude = result.lng;
      latitude = result.lat;
      zoom = 15;
    }}
  />

  <Map
    {longitude}
    {latitude}
    {zoom}
    height={600}
    theme="positron"
    credit="OpenFreeMap / OpenStreetMap contributors"
  >
    <!-- the MapLayer you added earlier stays as-is -->
  </Map>
```

Save and try it. Type a New York City address into the search box, pick a result from the dropdown and watch the map glide over to your block.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/geocoder-in-action.png"
  alt="The page showing a search box zoomed in after a location query"
  chromeTitle="The Great Trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

This is the reactive pattern you know from previous lessons, now connected to a map. The Geocoder updates three state variables. Those variables flow into the Map as properties. The Map reacts by animating to the new location.

## Part 4: Using data to style polygons

In 2015, NYC Parks ran its third decadal street tree census — a citizen science effort called [TreesCount!](https://www.nycgovparks.org/trees/treescount). Over 2,200 volunteers walked every block in the five boroughs with tablets, identifying every street tree they found and recording its species, trunk diameter, health, and exact location. They found 666,134 trees of 132 different species.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/treescount-homepage.png"
  alt="The NYC Parks TreesCount! 2015 homepage showing data visualizations about New York's street trees"
  chromeTitle="TreesCount! 2015"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nycgovparks.org/trees/treescount"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

One tree dominates that census: **the London planetree**. It's the tree Robert Moses, the unelected power broker who ran NYC Parks from 1934 to 1960, planted everywhere he built. Along his parkways. In his parks. Up and down street after street. Moses's favorite tree is still the most common street tree in New York — about one in ten of every street tree you pass is a London planetree, a direct artifact of one man's taste imposed on the city seventy years ago. The leaf in the NYC Parks logo is widely believed to depict one.

The Great Trees were easy: a tiny handful of points, nowhere near a megabyte, no trouble at all for MapLibre to render.

The London planetree is a different animal. There are around 70,000 of them in the 2015 census. If we tried to plot every one as an individual dot:

- The file that ships to each reader's browser would be roughly 8 to 10 megabytes. On classroom Wi-Fi that's a two-second wait; on a fading cell connection at the back of a bus, it's a spinner the reader gives up on.
- The browser would need to draw 70,000 shapes at once, which is doable but starts to stutter on older devices.
- Most of those dots would overlap with their neighbors anyway, so the visual payoff would be a blobby green haze — not the thing a reader can click or parse.

When a dataset is too big to ship as individual points, you have a few options. You could sample — show only every tenth tree. You could cluster — let MapLibre group nearby trees into expandable dots. Or you could **aggregate**: summarize the data into a smaller unit of geography before it ever leaves your machine.

Gothamist aggregated their shootings data to census blocks. We're going to aggregate our planetree data to a different administrative unit: the **Neighborhood Tabulation Area**, or NTA. NYC's Department of City Planning created NTAs to approximate the neighborhoods most New Yorkers recognize — "Park Slope-Gowanus," "Astoria-Ditmars-Steinway," "Washington Heights North" — by grouping census tracts into medium-sized statistical geographies. There are 195 of them, compared to 666,000 trees. The compression ratio is immense.

I've hosted the result of that aggregation at:

```
https://palewi.re/data/nyc-trees/planetrees-by-nta.geojson
```

Every one of the 195 NTA polygons arrives with a London planetree count already baked into its properties — along with richer context we'll get to in a moment.

### Load the second file

Fetch it alongside the Great Trees in `src/routes/+page.js`:

```js {emphasize-lines="3,4"}
export async function load({ fetch }) {
  const greatTrees = await fetch('https://palewi.re/data/nyc-trees/great-trees.geojson').then((r) => r.json());
  const planetrees = await fetch('https://palewi.re/data/nyc-trees/planetrees-by-nta.geojson').then((r) => r.json());
  return { greatTrees, planetrees };
}
```

And pull it out of the `data` prop in `+page.svelte`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
const greatTrees = data.greatTrees;
const planetrees = data.planetrees;
```

Pop open your browser console and inspect one of the NTA features:

```js
console.log(planetrees.features[0].properties);
```

You should see a property bag that looks roughly like this:

```js
{
  ntacode: "BK09",
  ntaname: "Brooklyn Heights-Cobble Hill",
  boroname: "Brooklyn",
  total_all_species: 5431,
  top_species: [
    { name: "honeylocust", count: 892 },
    { name: "London planetree", count: 734 },
    { name: "pin oak", count: 612 },
    { name: "Callery pear", count: 498 },
    { name: "littleleaf linden", count: 389 }
  ],
  count_total: 734,
  count_good: 521,
  count_fair: 176,
  count_poor: 37,
  count_small: 42,
  count_medium: 312,
  count_large: 380,
  rank: 2
}
```

Pause and absorb what's in there. Each NTA carries not just a single London planetree count but a whole dossier: how many total street trees it has of all species, the five most common species in rank order, and several pre-computed slices of its planetree population by health and by trunk size. The `rank` field is especially interesting — it's where the London planetree sits in the species leaderboard of this NTA. A `1` means Moses's tree wins here. A `2` or `3` means something else is in front.

This is what it looks like to pre-compute your story. A raw 70,000-tree dataset got squeezed into 195 feature-rich polygons before it ever touched the wire.

Now you can see the contrast between our two data files. Roughly a hundred and twenty points on one side. Fewer than two hundred polygons on the other. Both describe the same city, but in completely different ways. That shape difference is what drives the swap we're about to make.

### Swap the layer

Remove the Great Tree `MapLayer` from inside your `<Map>` block, and delete the matching `import greatTrees` line at the top of the script. Don't worry — it's not gone forever. We'll talk about how to bring it back at the end of class.

In its place, add two new `MapLayer`s: one that fills each NTA with a color based on its London planetree count, and one that draws the outlines between them.

A [choropleth map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choropleth_map) shades each region of a map by a numeric value. We're going to shade each NTA by its `count_total` — the number of London planetrees living inside it.

```svelte
<MapLayer
  id="nta-fill"
  type="fill"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'fill-color': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'count_total'],
      0, '#f0f7f4',
      250, '#95d5b2',
      1000, '#40916c',
      3000, '#1b4332'
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.7
  }}
/>
<MapLayer
  id="nta-outline"
  type="line"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'line-color': '#1b4332',
    'line-width': 0.5
  }}
/>
```

Reading the fill-color expression out loud: *interpolate linearly by the `count_total` property of each polygon. At 0 trees, use nearly-white. At 250 trees, a light green. At 1000, a medium green. At 3000 or more, a deep forest green.* MapLibre smooths between the stops automatically.

Also update the `Map`'s caption, since what it's showing has completely changed:

```svelte
<Map {longitude} {latitude} {zoom} theme="positron" caption="Every one of NYC's 70,000-plus London planetrees from the 2015 street tree census, aggregated to the neighborhood.">
```

Save. The amber dots are gone, and the city now fills with varying shades of green.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/choropleth.png"
  alt="A map of NYC with neighborhoods shaded from pale green to dark forest green by London planetree count. The darkest polygons concentrate along the Upper East Side, through brownstone Brooklyn, and out along the old parkways of Queens"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

You're now looking at Moses's New York. The darkest polygons are the NTAs where his tree is thickest on the ground — along the Upper East Side, through brownstone Brooklyn, ribboning out into Queens along the old parkways. The lightest polygons are the places his planting campaign never fully reached: the outermost edges of the city, the industrial corners, some of the newer developments.

### Add a popup

Let's make those polygons clickable. Add a `popup` to the fill layer that tells the reader what they're looking at:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="11-24"}
<MapLayer
  id="nta-fill"
  type="fill"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'fill-color': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'count_total'],
      0, '#f0f7f4', 250, '#95d5b2',
      1000, '#40916c', 3000, '#1b4332'
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.7
  }}
  popup={(feature) => {
    const p = feature.properties;
    const rankLine = p.rank === 1
      ? `<strong>#1 species in this neighborhood</strong>`
      : `London planetree ranks #${p.rank} — top species is ${p.top_species[0].name} (${p.top_species[0].count.toLocaleString()})`;
    return `
      <strong>${p.ntaname}</strong><br>
      ${p.count_total.toLocaleString()} London planetrees<br>
      ${rankLine}<br>
      <em>${p.count_good.toLocaleString()} Good · ${p.count_fair.toLocaleString()} Fair · ${p.count_poor.toLocaleString()} Poor</em>
    `;
  }}
/>
```

Save and click around. Each NTA now tells you three things: how many London planetrees it holds, where that tree ranks among all species growing there, and how those planetrees are faring healthwise.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/nta-popup.png"
  alt="A popup over a Brooklyn NTA reading 'Bedford-Stuyvesant (West): 1,847 London planetrees, London planetree ranks #2 — top species is honeylocust (2,104), and a health breakdown of 1,312 Good · 421 Fair · 114 Poor'"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That `rank` field is where the story gets interesting. Flick around the map and find the NTAs where Moses's tree is *not* #1. Some of the outer-borough neighborhoods — parts of Staten Island, corners of Eastern Queens — show honeylocust or pin oak as the champion instead, with the planetree trailing at #3 or #4. You are looking at the limits of one man's reach.

## Homework

### Task 1: Publish an interactive map

Find a point or polygon dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/). Build a page in your project that maps it with MapLibre, using the components in the template.

Your project must have:

- A `Map` component with data plotted from your chosen dataset
- A `Geocoder` for navigation
- A headline, introductory paragraph, and a source credit that present the map as a journalistic product, not just a demo.
- At least one feature we didn't cover in class, like a custom popup, a data filter or a more complex paint style.

Deploy to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to walk the class through your map and explain:

- The dataset you chose and why
- At least one editorial decision you made about what to show and what to leave out
